Date: Wed, 24 Mar 1993 14:23 EST
From: LEVY%LENNY@Venus.YCC.Yale.Edu
Subject: BANG: How I got my Extropian Name(s)

How I Got my Extropian Name(s)
by Simon! D. Levy a.k.a. S1m0n D. Levy

Well, Max was encouraging his friends to change their names to something
more Extropically powerful.  I am a pretty conservative sort, and feel
that I have earned the name Simon through extraordinary amounts of 
childhood peer harrassment (Simon Says, Simon Bar Sinister, Simon the Pieman, 
Simon the computer game) in a country where the name is comparatively rare.
Also, I identify both my first and last names with the Extropoid
values (integrity, hard work, skill) of my paternal grandfather Simon H. Levy,
who died shortly before I was born.  In fact, Levy is a name taken at
Ellis island by _his_ father, whose Russian Jewish name was apparently
too difficult for the immigration clerk to handle.  So, in fact, my last
name is Libertarian-Extropian: It comes from a man who left Russia to seek
economic opportunity (and probably to escape government-sactioned
antisemitism) and who was either then the victim of bureaucratic laziness,
or who voluntarily changed his name when he improved his circumstances.

As for the ! (bang), well, I wanted to make a change that would allow me to 
keep the pronounciation of my cherished given name, while also allowing
readers of _Extropy_ and the List (and later _Exponent_) to see my efforts
at positive change.  The bang, originally intended as a small parody of
the in-your-face exhuberance of names like More and Plus, ended up taking
on an etiological life of its own.  On the one hand, it is the symbol for
the factorial function, which is (conveniently) written n!, meaning
that the /n/ in my name is actually of some use too.  N! ("n factorial") 
increases explosively with n (2! = 2; 3! = 6; 4! = 24; 5! = 120; 6! = 720,...), 
so  I think I have captured some of the expansive flavor of names like More 
and Plus.  Furthermore, the fact that the function is recursively defined 
(n! = n * (n-1)!) makes it especially appealing to the computer nerd in me.

On the other hand, the ! is a symbol used for the rare lateral click (a.k.a.
lateral velaric ingressive) sound found in Zulu, Xhosa, and other
southern African languages, which pleases the linguist in me.  (To
make this sound, put the back of your tongue against the roof of your
mouth, suck in, and snap one side of your tongue down.  It should sound like the
noise made to get a horse moving.)  The sequence /n!/ cannot come at
the end of a word in any language I know of, so it's kind of a cheat, but
it's fun hearing people try to pronounce it.

There is also a dark side to the bang: It is the negating symbol in
C, a hacker "language of choice"; it turns any non-zero value into zero
(and zero into one).  Also, the usage of the bang as a delimiter in network 
addresses could cause me problems if I started using certain networks.

Recently, I have begun using the name S1m0n to reflect the fact that
I now spend most of my time programming binary-based digital computers.
Like Simon!, this name rhymes with... Simon.

