Date: Wed, 24 Mar 1993 15:25 EST
From: LEVY%LENNY@Venus.YCC.Yale.Edu
Subject: POLI: Noam Chomsky

Chomsky is a tough row to hoe.  On the one hand, he is a brilliant
mathematician who invented the Chomsky hierarchy of formal languages,
making an indelible contribution to both pure mathematics and computer
science.  On the other hand, he is a brilliant and controversial linguist 
who while almost singlehandedly inventing modern generative linguistics 
simultaneously denied the intellectual debt owed to his Structuralist 
predececssors, most notably his own teacher Zelig Harris; in fact, Chomsky
went out of his way to contrast the explanatory scientific power of
his framework with the putative vacuousness of Structuralist  "discovery
procedures" for analysis of linguistic data.  Like Socrates, he is
demonically clever at defeating anyone who seems to have a bulletproof
"intuitive" reason for attacking his approach.  Were in not for Chomsky,
I would venture to say, linguistics would remain a completely obscure
sub-discipline of anthropology and literary criticism, instead of being
a quasi-obscure field in its own right, with most major American universities
having a linguistics department.

Personally, I think that the Chomskyan approach has shed some light on the 
very specifically linguistic, counter-intuitive nature of human language, 
but has pretty much run its course, with Chomsky's adherents debating fine 
points that are important to them alone.  Chomsky's desire to make linguistics
a sub-field of psychology never really materialzed; most language psychologists
I know are either openly or quietly hostile to Chomsky's ideas, probably
because those ideas are based on a grammatical, rather than stastistical,
approach to language behavior.  I strongly suspect that, as computers
become more powerful, the really interesting work in linguistics and other
social sciences will come from complex systems theory, especially the
models used in artificial life.  For better or for worse, it is mostly
physicists and computer scientists who have the training to do this
sort of work; I think it's only a matter of time before such persons
start publishing papers on the origins of language universals and other
exciting topics that the Chomskyan framework could not address.

Chomsky's political views are equally tricky.  He calls himself a Libertarian
Socialist, which makes very little sense to me, and, as in his linguistic
work, he is intentionally inflammatory in what he says about world affairs.
I once saw him lecture on the refugee camps that Israel was running in
Lebanon in the early 80's; he was comparing the Israelis' treatment of Arabs
to the Nazis' treatment of Jews  in World War II.  This comparison achieved two
purposes: First, it enraged Jews everywhere, especially those
who survived the Holocaust and see Israel as the Jews' only chance
of escaping total annihilation.  Second, it made a generally pro-Israel
Jew like me think about whether, in preserving a Jewish State, Israelis
must become "goyish" in their treatment of others.  

I am inclined to agree with an earlier post that Chomsky's true colors 
can be discerned by the fact that he writes for Z, the last enclave of 
die-hard campus pinkos who think that the Soviet Union's bad rap was mostly 
a result of American media propagandizing.  For lack of anything better to
read while waiting in a university library last month, I picked up a 
copy of that mag and read a Chomsky article on the U.S. "invasion"
of Somalia.  As usual, I found myself agreeing with many of Chomsky's
points (e.g., that the military operation was too little too late and
had a least of part of its motivation in the armed forces' desire to
look necessary after the Cold War), and hating his style.  For example,
economists only had their affiliations mentioned if they were, like
Chomsky, at MIT; this kind of in-crowd cheerleading is also common among his
linguistics colleagues and students.  Finally, he dismissed Friedmanist
free-market economics as "having failed at every turn," but gave none
of the jillion references he uses to support an idea with which he agrees.

In conclusion, I would say that no matter how you feel about Chomsky,
nothing he says can be dismissed lightly.

-- Simon

