Date: Wed, 24 Mar 93 13:49:37 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: PHIL: Identity, Transformation, Uploading

Max has written a thoughful piece, clearly part of a large carefully
considered body of thought.  But I am somewhat uncomfortable with
framing the central question as something like trying to figure out
just what we mean, or want to mean, by "same identity" for a person.

To pose things this way suggests there is a relatively objective
answer, which we just have to search for.  However, it seems that
instead the central question is really "what do I want?", i.e., what
kind of future, with what kinds of future beings, do I want to
contribute to?

Do I want beings with my memories, my personality, my beliefs, my
sexual tastes, my facial expression, etc., and how am I willing to
trade these off against one another, and against the number of each,
their relative wealth, etc.?  People have always had to deal with some
of these issues, when they decided how much to stay "youthful" and
perhaps irresponsible, and how much to "mature" in various ways.  But
the prospects for much more unbundling of these traits raises lots
more interesting issues which folks should consider, and I'm glad Max
is doing so.

But talking about "identity" instead seems too indirect, and not
entirely honest.  Mark Miller wants a future that will credit him, so
he defines identity in terms of credit.  Max wants to change a lot, so
he defines identity in terms of continuity, and criticizes similarity
based conceptions of identity.  The bottom line seems to be that
people seem to want to preserve both "I want the future to be X", and
"I want the future to have lots of *me*", and are willing to slide the
meaning of "me" to maintain both these statements.  

For example, Max says

>A view of identity or personal continuity promoting the belief
>that change brings death clearly will not foster extropian commitment to
>self-transformation and personal growth!  Of course, an extropian with this
>view might choose self-sacrifice, self-destruction in favor of their superior
>successor.  Still, such a self-conception seems open to abuse.  Fortunately,
>not only is it anti-extropic, it is false.

"false" is a bit strong, and all these concepts of identity seem "open
to abuse".  Why not just talk about what kinds of futures we want, and
not pretend we must mean the same thing by "identity"?

Robin Hanson

