Perry writes:
>Irrespective of your solution, I'll note the problem exists now. You
>can be mailed a threat anonymously using ordinary U.S. Mail, and you
>can be forced into an anonymous payment scheme pretty easily. The
>second part isn't obvious, but its true. It will even work with
>extreme surveilance -- lets say extortionist can't himself receive
>payment safely -- he could still easily send someone a note saying
>"contribute $10,000 to ExI or I blow your wife's head off".

Well this is a point worth noting, but the set of people both willing
to engage in extortion and holding a strong interest in some charity
where they can anonymously verify who donated is probably pretty
small.  It seems clear to me that the ability to get money to
*yourself* by extorting would certainly increase the set of people
interested, and therefore raises the concern of more such activity.

Of course if you could show that there are many people who are
interesting in charity extortion, but are now prevented from doing so
by the risk of getting caught carrying out their threat, then that
would show that just allowing anonymous payment shouldn't raise the 
extortion rate greatly.  

Robin Hanson

