Date: Wed, 24 Mar 93 14:08:09 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Chomsky, Conspiracies, and Jews

Whether one agrees with him or not, the world is a better place for having
Chomsky in it, in my opinion. I enjoy his radio interviews and find his
stuff prevocative. 

Though I'm a libertarian/anarchocapitalist, I also "fellow travel" with
some mostly leftist "conspiracy theory" types (found on public broadcasting
radio stations across the nation). A particular favorite of mine is Dave
Emory, a former student of Mae Brussell, who originates a radio broadcast
in the Bay Area. (For Bay Aryans, Sunday nights from 7 to 11 on KFJC, 89.7
and Monday nights from 9 to 10:30 on KKUP, 91.5)

Typical keywords: CIA, NSA, Wackenhut, Danny Casolaro, Reinhard Gelhlen,
Nazis, U.S. funding of Hitler, Allen Dulles, Bank for International
Settlements, BCCI, Banco Nazionale del Lavoro, Gladio, P2, Italian fascism,
terrorism, links to U.S. intelligence agencies, invasion of Panama, Iraq,
Iran, Cabazon Indians, arms deals, biological weapons, mind control,
Satanism, Zodiac killer, and so on.

Fun stuff. I usually tape his broadcasts and then replay them to put me a
pleasant state of paranoia and altered consciosness.

These points, which sound like paranoid conspiracy theories to most folks,
actually relate to the many abuses of state power these last several
decades. While the socialist countries are by no means any better (they are
in fact much, much worse) in abuse of government power, it is important
that we expose government crimes whenever possible.

BTW, the leftists who are often associated with these theories are
sometimes allied with right-wing groups (like the Liberty Lobby, and even
the Birchers). Libertarian ideas may someday find a fertile ground here
(perhaps after disillusionment with Clinton sets in).

Regarding Simon's (or Simon!'s or S1mOn's) points:

>I once saw him lecture on the refugee camps that Israel was running in
>Lebanon in the early 80's; he was comparing the Israelis' treatment of Arabs
>to the Nazis' treatment of Jews  in World War II.  This comparison achieved two
>purposes: First, it enraged Jews everywhere, especially those
>who survived the Holocaust and see Israel as the Jews' only chance
>of escaping total annihilation.  Second, it made a generally pro-Israel
>Jew like me think about whether, in preserving a Jewish State, Israelis
>must become "goyish" in their treatment of others.  

I have no desire to start a flame war over Israel and its right to exist
here, so take my words as just my opinion. (By the way, it's interesting to
me that in 8 months of reading the list, I can't recall seeing the issue of
Israel coming up.)

Concentrating Jews in a land they hadn't lived in for thousands of years by
kicking Palestinians off the land they *had* been living in does not seem a
"fair" solution. (Yes, I know many Palestinians fled their land during the
war in 1948. But since when does fleeing a war zone cause ownership of a
home or farm to change hands?)

And I doubt the survival of Jews in the state of Israel is really any
better than elsewhere. A few nukes delivered to Tel Aviv and Haifa will
kill a lot more Jews than I can imagine ever dying in a new Holocaust. Call
this a practicality argument.

And Simon's point about "goyish" behavior is right on, except I'd
characterize it as "statist" behavior. Israel is a state, a highly statist
state (controlled economy, militarily disciplined, etc.--though with far
more freedoms, needless to say, than its Arab and Palestinian neighbors).
By locating the Jews in a hostile locale, the U.N. essentially played a
kind of trick on the Jewish survivors of the Holocaust--they caused Jews to
form their own state and imitate many of the structures that led to the
rise of Hitler in Germany. (Understand that I am in no way imputing Nazi
ideas to Israeli leaders--mostly they seem pretty reasonable. Nor am I
forecasting that Israel will ever follow the Nazi path...refugee camps are
a far cry from death camps.)

But since I am not religious (not even NeoPagan), and don't generally think
of ethnic heritage as being all that important, I've never understood the
importance placed in announcing one's "Jewishness" or supporting a
particular religious state, be it Israel or Iran.

Israel as a nation-state bores me. If Jewish heritage is a powerful set of
memes, let it flourish in diverse locations, not crammed into a tiny part
of the desert.

-Tim May

