Date: Wed, 24 Mar 93 16:30:26 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ECON/CRYPT: Crypto-Extortion

Robin Hanson writes:

>An influential friend whom I respect is seriously concerned that the
>possibility of cryptographic support for extortion should make us
>seriously question the desirability of wider use of cryptography.  So
>I did the following analysis to see if we can find good enough ways to
>discourage such extortion.

Yes, crypto will bring on a Brave New World.

Use of anonymity and untraceable digital cash for unsavory uses, such as
extortion and contract murders (think about it for a minute), has been on
my mind for several years. Since beginning to talk about "crypto anarchy"
in late 1987, I've talked with several folks about these issues. (Phil
Salin, Mark Miller, Dean Tribble, Marc Stiegler, Eric Drexler, Chris
Peterson, Gayle Pergamit, David Chaum, Jim Bennett, Dave Ross, all by 1988,
and many more since.)

First, there are already several direct analogies. Perry Metzger has
already mentioned postal mail threats and telephone calls. (Not the origin
of the terms "blackmail" and "poison pen letters" for evidence this is not
a new problem.) Likewise, our modern society is open to many debilitating
attacks.

For example, a single person can shut down the entire U.S. table grape
market merely by injecting a few grapes with cyanide (the Chilean grape
story of a few years back). Maybe extortion, maybe unionizing activities,
maybe an axe to grind, maybe something else. Similar cases have hit bottled
medicines (Tylenol case), meat, etc. In an open, scattered society, such
things are possible. Anonymous bomb threats recently emptied the Empire
State Building. And so on. Life goes on.

Second, strong crypto cannot easily be stopped. Check out the "Cypherpunks"
list for debate on this, or sci.crypt. The "genie is out of the bottle."
While I applaud Robin's interest in this scenario, and encourage him to
continue studying it, we should understand that the "dark side" of crypto
will probably not be sufficient to stop strong crypto from spreading.

Third, I *do* expect some people will explicitly adopt a "I don't read
messages unless I know who they're from" strategy, in much the same way
publishers have to be very careful not to read manuscripts sent to them
unsolicited.  This will still not stop extortion, as the extortionist can
find many channels for getting the initial message to his victim (letters,
phone calls, a message tied to a rock through his window, etc.). But it
makes some targets harder to reach and thus damps the extortion threat
somewhat.

Just as a policy of "no negotation with kidnappers" is not perfect, so too
will this strategy not be perfect.

Fourth, more crypto privacy may mean knowing how much money a potential
victim has is harder to achieve. People may live more sheltered lives,
perhaps behind walls (as in Arab cultures). This still does not end the
threat.

Fifth, and most speculatively, the threats in the "real world" may
accelerate a conversion to more interactions in cyberspace, where physical
identities ("true names") are well-secured. This will not happen anytime
soon, of course.

>The basic scenario is that everyone *says* up front that they will not
>give into extortion, but this claim may not be credible.  So an
>extortionist sends an anonymous threat to someone, asking that a
>certain amount of untraceable digital money be sent to them by a
>certain date, demonstrating in some way that the extortionists knows
>where they live or can be harmed, and claiming that if they are not
>paid something very bad will happen to this victim.
>
>The extortionist also makes reference to an extortionist rating
>agency, who offers information relevant for estimating the chance that
>this extortionist will follow through on a threat, and on the
>additional number of similar threats the victim may expect if they
>give in to this one.  If the victim's normal mechanisms for preventing
>him/her from giving in have failed for some reason, the victim then
>may give in if the ransom asked is less than the expected harm if
>he/she doesn't give in.

...equations and "formalism" elided....

>The technology of commitment determines how g is a function of R.
>Larger payments are harder to hide from one's family and associates,
>who can help one enforcer prior commitments to not give in.  Larger R
>also increases n,m because others are more likley to learn of your
>having given in.  Ensuring that digital cash transfers above a certain
>amount are known to associates provides a way to commit to not give in.
>
>The technology of filtering determines r.  If one could send all
>unsolicited messages to an extortion filter, which promises to filter
>out all such messages from your view, r might be made low.  Or such
>messages might always be forwarded to an associate in order to reduce g.

Yes, one can always hire others to screen messages, to approve all
transfers of wealth, etc. Sort of like a "power of attorney" or a
mail-screening service.

...Robin's creative ideas on constructing an extortion service elided...

>Without a plausible scenario for how such reputations could be created
>and communicated, extortion seems less of a problem; potential victims
>would estimate n,m so high that they would not think it in their
>interest to give in, even if able.  This is hopeful, but before
>breathing too easy we should work harder searching for such scenarios.

Nothing forces either the extortionist or the victim to go through these
hypothetical extortion rating agencies...if in fact such agencies make
extortion less profitable or even not feasible, why would they be used?

An extortionist can always "secretly" contact his victim, make a threat,
demand a payment, and collect his payment all without contacting anyone or
any entity besides his victim. In other words, the way it's usually done
today.

As for the idea of requiring a second party to authorize any transactions
(to provide a way to stop extortion payments), this can be bypassed in the
usual creative ways (called "money laundering"--many creative schemes can
be found). The extortionist may agree to periodic payments below the
"threshold" that Robin mentioned in his original message, exactly analogous
to how blackmail victims keep paying and paying. And so on. (But I agree
with Robin that this seems to be an avenue to pursue.)

Though I favor strong crypto and even crypto anarchy, I am less confident
than Robin that these markets in assassinations and extortion can be
suppressed.

-Tim May


