Date: Wed, 24 Mar 93 17:24:00 CST
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: PHIL: Identity, Transformation, Uploading

Robin Hanson writes, regarding Max More's thoughts on identity:

> The bottom line seems to be that
> people seem to want to preserve both "I want the future to be X", and
> "I want the future to have lots of *me*", and are willing to slide the
> meaning of "me" to maintain both these statements.  

Identity theory does have purpose, at least according to our
current common world-views; it helps us make sense out of the
way we move through time, and helps us make descisions about
how to treat changes in ourselves and others [or so the story
goes!].

If anything, *our desires about X* are what do the sliding,
depending on how we understand our identities.  When Max and
Mark Miller and others make statements like:

> >A view of identity or personal continuity promoting the belief
> >that change brings death clearly will not foster extropian commitment to
> >self-transformation and personal growth!

I believe that what's really meant is that the intuition about
identity being expressed *depends on* and thus *needs to be
expressed in relation to* things like commitment to self-
transformation and personal growth.  I justify this by noting
that there are two classes of identity theories:

  1) Those that are obviously incorrect (for example, if
  I tried to claim that my identity follows the hat I'm
  wearing now).

  2) Those that are arguable.

That is, there can't be a single rigorously correct identity theory,
because then the thing it's a theory *of* would have to be spelled
out in exact detail, and many people would then say, "that's not
what *I* mean by identity!"

So, theories of type two are (purportedly) judged on the basis of
how well they explain identity-phenomena -- taking into account
the past and (as Max has pointed out) the future.

More importantly, though, is to note, as Max implies in the above
snippet, that identity theories of type two are really best seen
as "memetic viruses" -- infecting the hearer with a world view.
Logic and rhetoric are good transport mechanisms.  When Max
and his fellow philosophers argue and "disprove" each other's
ideas about identity, they're not so much explaining as *defining*
what you are and what you'll be.

derek VRRRROOOOOOOOMMMMMM!
so watch your rear -- those philosophers have done a pretty good
job of covering their intentions with images of bumbling and
angels-on-pinheads...

