Subject: META: Is an armed society a polite society?

OK, here is the final version of the Polite Society rules, should
anyone still be interested.  Folks may now officialy join, though I
won't quibble if folks who claimed to join before the final rules were
out now reafirm their membership.  

Robin Hanson, Polite Society Member Since 3/93 - 0 Hits, 0 Shots

P.S. I also now officially join the Accountable Society, and have
applied for and been accepted as a member to the Invitational Polite
Society.

----------------------------------------------------------
         AN INVITATION TO JOIN THE POLITE SOCIETY

  "An armed society is a polite society" - Anon

Tired of flaming and discourteous manners in email and netnews?  Why not
join the "Polite Society"?  Each member is "armed" and may "shoot" any
other member they deem has made an especially impolite post.  After a
shot, neither member may post for one week to any newsgroup, email list,
BBS, etc.  While not a perfect indicator, longstanding members with few
hits have passed at least one substantial test of their politeness.

This society is not intended as a game, or as a way to play "cowboy".  To
make this work, a substantial majority of members must restrain themselves
and shoot only for clear impoliteness.  Members should consider anyone
doing otherwise to be extremely impolite, and act accordingly.

Frequent posters suffer more per shot, but in general make more impolite
posts.  Society members accept this, accepting "lurkers" as equal members.

FINE POINTS:
- Any person may join by so declaring in a signature to a post.
- Members must use their true names when acting as members, and may
  not join under more than one name.
- One may not shoot until having been a member for two weeks.
- A "shot" is a post submitted to the same newsgroups, lists, etc.
  as the offending post, and it must be forwarded, with a copy of the
  offending post, directly to the person hit.  
- A shot post should quote offending passages, include a paragraph 
  (20-200 words) explaining how the offending post is impolite (rather 
  than just wrong), and include or refer to an example of what should 
  have been said in place of the offensive quotes.  
- For one week after being hit, a person may not post messages to any
  email list, newsgroup, BBS, or analogous communication media.
- Exceptions are made for a short message explaining one's upcoming
  absence, and for posts required to maintain one's job.
- It takes one month to "reload" for another shot.
- You can't ever shoot the same person twice, be shot twice for the
  same offending post, or shoot someone out from a hit.
- A post whose subject line includes an "(impolite)" warning, or which
  appears on a list, etc., explicitly labeled as such, may not be shot at.
- After getting ten hits one is expelled from the Society for two years.
- Members grant permission for shot posts to be freely distributed, 
  including their offensive quotes.
- When asked, a member must admit their membership, tell the year and
  month they joined, and the number of times they have shot or been hit.
- All this information should be included in any membership declarations.
  For example: "Polite Society Member Since 3/93 - 0 Hits, 1 Shot"
- To allow verification, each member must record who shot them, who
  they shot, on what dates, and forward this info to whomever asks.  
- If two members dispute how these rules should apply to some case, they
  should pick a neutral member to arbitrate.  Failing this, members should 
  shoot any member whose violations are so clear and substantial as to be
  considered impolite.  

This invitation may be freely distributed, and is copyright Robin Hanson 1993.



